Brute Force
-----------
Idea:
- Try every possible buy day.
- For each buy day, find the next profitable sell day.
- Repeat until the end of the array.
- This approach repeatedly scans the remaining array.

Algorithm:
1. For every day i:
   - Find a future day j where prices[j] > prices[i].
   - Add the maximum possible profit.
2. Continue until the last day.

Time Complexity: O(n²)
Space Complexity: O(1)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int maxProfit(vector<int>& prices) {
        int n = prices.size();
        int profit = 0;
        int i = 0;

        while (i < n - 1) {
            while (i < n - 1 && prices[i] >= prices[i + 1])
                i++;

            if (i == n - 1) break;

            int buy = prices[i++];

            while (i < n && prices[i] >= prices[i - 1])
                i++;

            int sell = prices[i - 1];

            profit += sell - buy;
        }

        return profit;
    }
};
```

Better Solution
---------------
Idea:
- Every increasing adjacent pair contributes to the final answer.
- If today's price is greater than yesterday's, take the profit immediately.
- The sum of all positive differences equals the maximum profit.

Algorithm:
1. Initialize profit = 0.
2. Traverse from index 1.
3. If prices[i] > prices[i-1]:
   - profit += prices[i] - prices[i-1]
4. Return profit.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int maxProfit(vector<int>& prices) {
        int profit = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i < prices.size(); i++) {
            if (prices[i] > prices[i - 1])
                profit += prices[i] - prices[i - 1];
        }

        return profit;
    }
};
```

Optimal Solution
----------------
No intermediate improvement exists. The greedy approach above is already the optimal solution.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)

Interview Notes
---------------
- Unlike Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock I, multiple transactions are allowed.
- Greedy works because every increasing sequence:
  - Buy at the start.
  - Sell at the end.
  - is equivalent to adding every positive daily increase.
- Example:
  - Prices = [1,2,3,4]
  - Profit = (2-1) + (3-2) + (4-3) = 3
  - Same as buying at 1 and selling at 4.
- Edge Cases:
  - Single day.
  - Strictly decreasing prices (profit = 0).
  - Strictly increasing prices.
  - Repeated equal prices.
- Follow-up:
  - What if only one transaction is allowed?
    - Use the minimum-price approach (LeetCode 121).
  - What if there's a transaction fee or cooldown?
    - Use Dynamic Programming.
- This greedy observation is one of the most frequently asked stock interview patterns.
